\newlabel{tab:noiseN}{{5}{4}{How the gradient noise and the cost depend on the particle count. The noise is the scatter of one seed's gradient divided by the gradient's size, measured with 16 seeds in pass 1. At the circle it falls as $N^{-0.46}$, close to the ideal $N^{-1/2}$.\relax }{table.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Whether the particle count biases the result. Each row measures the same fixed shape with two particle counts on the same unseen seeds. The drag is given per $10^5$ particles so that the counts can be compared. Two search directions count as the same when they differ by no more than their noise.\relax }}{5}{table.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:bias}{{6}{5}{Whether the particle count biases the result. Each row measures the same fixed shape with two particle counts on the same unseen seeds. The drag is given per $10^5$ particles so that the counts can be compared. Two search directions count as the same when they differ by no more than their noise.\relax }{table.caption.9}{}}
